<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zapotec civilization</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zapotecs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zapotec civilization arose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-oaxaca">Valley of Oaxaca</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, where from about 500 BCE Monte Albán became one of the first cities and states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotecs">Zapotecs</a> developed one of the earliest writing systems in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, built temple and palace complexes and buried their nobles in painted tombs. After Monte Albán declined they lived in smaller kingdoms such as Zaachila, Mitla and Tehuantepec, and Zapotec languages are still spoken today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zapotec-civilization.jpg" alt="Zapotec civilization" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yavidaxiu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zapotecos.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some compounds were occupied by migrants: the Tlailotlacan district on the western edge housed people from the Valley of Oaxaca who kept <b>Zapotec-style</b> tombs and pottery for centuries, and the so-called Merchants’ Barrio in the east contained pottery from the Gulf Coast and the Maya lowlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Mitla, Lyobaa in <b>Zapotec</b>, was occupied for many centuries, but it grew into a major centre after the decline of Monte Albán, from around 900.</blockquote><a href="/state/mitla">Mitla</a></li><li><blockquote>The early Danzantes carvings show slain or sacrificed captives, and Building J lists conquered places, among the earliest evidence of <b>Zapotec</b> writing and expansion.</blockquote><a href="/state/monte-alban">Monte Albán</a></li><li><blockquote>Zaachila, called Teozapotlan by the Nahuas, emerged as the main <b>Zapotec</b> royal seat in the Valley of Oaxaca in the Postclassic period, from around 1100, filling the gap left by the decline of Monte Albán.</blockquote><a href="/state/zaachila">Zaachila</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 15th century the <b>Zapotec</b> king Cocijoeza of Zaachila conquered the Isthmus of Tehuantepec from Mixe, Zoque and Huave peoples and moved his royal seat there, around 1490, gaining control of the trade route between central Mexico and Soconusco.</blockquote><a href="/state/zapotec-kingdom-of-tehuantepec">Zapotec Kingdom of Tehuantepec</a></li></ul></section>
